--- !u!114 &-4931289588919842803
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 935228c0fd2628c4db5028980babef0b, type: 3}
  m_Name: NarrativeParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.NarrativeParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "Explore the scene to see how white balancing is utilized in this template.\r\n\r\n1. In the <i>Hierarchy,</i> select <b>Lighting</b> > <b>Volumes</b> > <b>Volume Room 3 Sitting.</b>\r\n\n2. Toggle the <b>White Balance</b> volume override on and off.\n\nNotice that the White Balance in this scenario applies a light blue color filter to the image, to counterbalance the strong orange color cast from the lamps above the chairs.\r\n\r\n3. Experiment with the <b>Temperature</b> slider to increase the level of blue or red in the image.\n\r\n4. Undo this change.\r\n\n\n<b>Expert Tips</b>\n\r\n\u2022 Artificial lighting often falls on the warm side of the color temperature range, and white balance is a way to adjust the perception of the color White in a Scene.\r\n\r\n\u2022 The opposite effect can be applied to an outdoor Scene with a blue Sky, which tends to have a bluer color cast, and therefore requires warmer <b>White Balance</b> settings.\r"
